1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,562
Diya holds a digital
thermometer under her tongue.

2
00:00:03,562 --> 00:00:07,271
One minute later, it beeps and
shows thirty seven degrees.

3
00:00:07,621 --> 00:00:09,881
But a thermometer cannot feel anything.

4
00:00:09,881 --> 00:00:12,300
So how does it turn heat into a number?

5
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:14,790
The secret is a transducer.

6
00:00:15,140 --> 00:00:20,166
A transducer is a device that changes
energy from one form into another.

7
00:00:20,516 --> 00:00:25,073
In electronics, it turns a physical
quantity, like heat, light, sound or

8
00:00:25,073 --> 00:00:27,414
pressure, into an electrical signal.

9
00:00:27,414 --> 00:00:29,039
Or the other way round.

10
00:00:29,689 --> 00:00:32,198
A microphone is an input transducer.

11
00:00:32,198 --> 00:00:35,031
It turns sound into an electrical signal.

12
00:00:35,381 --> 00:00:37,989
A loudspeaker is an output transducer.

13
00:00:37,989 --> 00:00:41,189
It turns the electrical
signal back into sound.

14
00:00:41,839 --> 00:00:44,752
Now let's open the
thermometer on the breadboard.

15
00:00:45,102 --> 00:00:46,756
Inside is a thermistor.

16
00:00:46,756 --> 00:00:50,630
That is a resistor whose resistance
changes with temperature.

17
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:55,782
It sits in series with a fixed ten
kilo ohm resistor, across a five volt

18
00:00:55,782 --> 00:00:56,682
supply.

19
00:00:56,463 --> 00:00:59,593
We read the voltage
across the fixed resistor.

20
00:00:59,942 --> 00:01:04,638
At room temperature, twenty five
degrees, the thermistor is also ten kilo

21
00:01:04,638 --> 00:01:05,538
ohms.

22
00:01:05,207 --> 00:01:09,028
So the output is half the
supply, two point five volts.

23
00:01:09,378 --> 00:01:11,340
Now Diya's mouth warms it up.

24
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:12,638
Watch the readout.

25
00:01:12,638 --> 00:01:16,425
The temperature climbs, and the
thermistor's resistance falls.

26
00:01:16,775 --> 00:01:19,822
More current flows, so
the electrons speed up.

27
00:01:19,822 --> 00:01:22,577
And the output voltage on the scope rises.

28
00:01:22,927 --> 00:01:27,096
The chip inside reads that voltage
and shows it as a temperature.

29
00:01:27,096 --> 00:01:28,716
Heat in, voltage out.

30
00:01:29,367 --> 00:01:32,264
A transducer turns the world into signals.

31
00:01:33,064 --> 00:01:35,093
Why does the resistance fall?

32
00:01:35,093 --> 00:01:37,802
A thermistor is made of a semiconductor.

33
00:01:38,152 --> 00:01:42,983
Heat frees more charge carriers inside
it, so current flows more easily.

34
00:01:42,983 --> 00:01:47,215
This type is called a negative
temperature coefficient thermistor.

35
00:01:47,865 --> 00:01:50,125
Now let's find the output voltage.

36
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:52,436
This circuit is a voltage divider.

37
00:01:52,786 --> 00:01:55,925
The same current flows
through both resistors.

38
00:01:55,925 --> 00:01:59,995
I equals the supply voltage
divided by the total resistance.

39
00:02:00,695 --> 00:02:04,426
The output is the voltage
across the fixed resistor.

40
00:02:04,426 --> 00:02:06,363
V out equals I times R.

41
00:02:07,063 --> 00:02:08,757
Put the two together.

42
00:02:08,757 --> 00:02:14,025
V out equals the supply voltage, times
R, divided by R plus the thermistor

43
00:02:14,025 --> 00:02:14,971
resistance.

44
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:18,511
Resistances in ohms, voltages in volts.

45
00:02:18,511 --> 00:02:22,141
The ratio decides what
share of the supply you get.

46
00:02:22,791 --> 00:02:23,966
Worked example.

47
00:02:23,966 --> 00:02:28,327
At thirty seven degrees, the
thermistor drops to six kilo ohms.

48
00:02:28,677 --> 00:02:32,944
V out equals five, times
ten, divided by ten plus six.

49
00:02:33,544 --> 00:02:36,186
That is three point one two five volts.

50
00:02:36,186 --> 00:02:37,863
Up from two point five.

51
00:02:37,863 --> 00:02:39,687
That change is the signal.

52
00:02:40,337 --> 00:02:43,401
Some transducers do not
need a supply at all.

53
00:02:43,401 --> 00:02:45,366
They make their own voltage.

54
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:47,734
These are called active transducers.

55
00:02:48,084 --> 00:02:51,975
A thermocouple makes a small
voltage when its junction is heated.

56
00:02:51,975 --> 00:02:54,529
A solar cell makes a voltage from light.

57
00:02:54,879 --> 00:02:58,542
And a piezoelectric crystal makes
a voltage when you squeeze it.

58
00:02:59,192 --> 00:03:03,687
Others only change their resistance,
so they need an outside supply.

59
00:03:03,687 --> 00:03:05,715
These are passive transducers.

60
00:03:06,065 --> 00:03:10,303
The thermistor, the light dependent
resistor and the strain gauge are all

61
00:03:10,303 --> 00:03:11,203
passive.

62
00:03:11,619 --> 00:03:13,374
Transducers are everywhere.

63
00:03:13,724 --> 00:03:17,506
A light dependent resistor
switches street lights on at dusk.

64
00:03:17,506 --> 00:03:20,949
A strain gauge inside a
weighing scale feels the load.

65
00:03:21,299 --> 00:03:26,232
And in hospitals, the ultrasound
scanner uses a piezoelectric crystal to

66
00:03:26,232 --> 00:03:28,469
send and receive sound waves.

67
00:03:29,119 --> 00:03:30,584
Now, the exam trap.

68
00:03:30,934 --> 00:03:34,769
Many students think every
transducer needs a power supply.

69
00:03:34,769 --> 00:03:35,840
It does not.

70
00:03:36,190 --> 00:03:40,078
Active transducers like the
thermocouple make their own voltage.

71
00:03:40,078 --> 00:03:43,674
Only passive ones, like the
thermistor, need a supply.

72
00:03:44,324 --> 00:03:45,955
Where is this asked?

73
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:47,586
In PUC board papers.

74
00:03:47,586 --> 00:03:52,469
Define a transducer, tell active from
passive with examples, or explain

75
00:03:52,469 --> 00:03:53,749
one application.

76
00:03:54,399 --> 00:03:55,299
Try this.

77
00:03:55,283 --> 00:04:00,334
A light dependent resistor and a four
kilo ohm resistor are in series across

78
00:04:00,334 --> 00:04:01,271
six volts.

79
00:04:01,271 --> 00:04:06,161
In the dark, the voltage across the
four kilo ohm resistor is one point

80
00:04:06,161 --> 00:04:07,098
two volts.

81
00:04:07,098 --> 00:04:10,911
What is the resistance of
the light dependent resistor?

82
00:04:16,311 --> 00:04:17,211
C.

83
00:04:16,858 --> 00:04:18,632
Sixteen kilo ohms.

84
00:04:18,982 --> 00:04:20,598
First, the current.

85
00:04:20,598 --> 00:04:26,102
One point two volts divided by four kilo
ohms is zero point three milliamperes.

86
00:04:26,452 --> 00:04:30,614
The rest of the supply drops across
the light dependent resistor.

87
00:04:30,614 --> 00:04:33,966
Six minus one point two
is four point eight volts.

88
00:04:34,316 --> 00:04:39,910
So its resistance is four point eight
divided by zero point three milliamperes.

89
00:04:39,910 --> 00:04:41,403
Sixteen kilo ohms.

90
00:04:42,053 --> 00:04:43,577
Screenshot this card.

91
00:04:46,777 --> 00:04:49,571
Next, component symbols
and identification.

92
00:04:49,571 --> 00:04:53,532
How do you tell a capacitor
from a resistor just by looking?
